.TH INTERFERE 1
.SH NAME
interfere \- find colliding parts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B interfere
[
.B \-j
.I n
]
.I assembly.st
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Interfere
prints a line for each pair of colliding parts of the whole assembly,
subassemblies too, each by its full name (as p1/knob/cap,
.IR place (1)):
.PP
.RS
.I "name name"
.B points
.I n
.RE
.PP
The material of each part is sampled on a 1 mm grid of its flat faces,
at three depths.
A sample collides if it lies inside the other part, 1 um or more from
its faces; parts that only touch do not collide.
.I N
is the number of colliding samples, counted up to a limit.
The pairs are counted by
.I n
processes, 4 by default, with the same output for any
.IR n .
.PP
A pair with a solid part (from a stream, see
.IR place (1))
is checked on the exact solids of both instead.
Pairs whose boxes stand apart are skipped.
The faces and the edges of each solid are sampled on their exact
surfaces and curves, each sample 1 um inside its own solid, and a
sample that lies inside the other solid collides.
Every sample lies on its face, not on the mesh past a curved edge, so
solids that only touch do not collide on any surface, as a cone in a
countersink of its angle.
Samples lie at most
.I h
apart, the lesser of 0.5 mm and half the thinner of the two parts (the
sheet of a folded part, or the thinnest wall of a solid between two
faces turned away from each other), but only where a face of the other
solid comes within 0.1 mm: elsewhere a piece of a face lies wholly
inside the other solid or wholly outside it, and one sample tells which,
and so does one for a flat piece that the faces near it, all flat,
leave on one side.
.PP
So a collision is found wherever an edge of one solid runs inside the
other for more than
.IR h ,
or where the part of a face of one inside the other holds a disk of
diameter 1.2
.IR h :
in particular wherever one part goes into or through another, however
thin either is.
.SH SEE ALSO
place(1)
.SH BUGS
Between two folded parts, an overlap thinner than the 1 mm grid may be
missed.
Between solids, a collision thinner than about 2 um may be missed, and
the sampling grid stops at 0.1 mm for walls thinner than 0.2 mm.
